      en: "Kumamoto Castle in Kumamoto Prefecture is one of Japan's three premier castles, known for its imposing stone walls and three-tiered main keep. Built in 1607 by Kato Kiyomasa, it features innovative defensive structures like the 'musha-gaeshi' (samurai-repelling) walls. The castle withstood the 1877 Satsuma Rebellion siege for 53 days. In the 2016 Kumamoto earthquake, significant damage occurred, including collapsed stone walls and roof tiles. Restoration has been ongoing, with full completion expected in 2023. The main keep stands 49 meters tall. The grounds cover 41 hectares, including the Chausuyama park area. Kumamoto Castle is designated a National Treasure and attracts about 3 million visitors annually.",
