      en: "Kan'ei-ji Temple in Taitō, Tokyo, was founded in 1625 by the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu as the Tokugawa clan's mortuary temple. During the Edo period, it was a vast temple complex with over 30 buildings. Much of it was destroyed in WWII, but the five-story pagoda (Gojunoto), built in 1639, and the main hall (Kondo), rebuilt in 1879, remain. The pagoda, at 36 meters tall, is a designated Important Cultural Property. The temple grounds are part of Ueno Park and include Shinobazu Pond. Kan'ei-ji is also home to the Ueno Daibutsu, a bronze Buddha statue. The temple attracts visitors for its historical significance and serene atmosphere amidst the urban park.",
